# Docs before implementation

The detailed wiki lives in `docs/`. Package READMEs stay short and point at it.
A new feature or behavior change updates that wiki and its tests before any
production code.

## Order

1. Add or update the page for the concern. Set **Status** to `planned`. Link it
   from that area's `overview.md` and from `docs/README.md`.
2. Add a row to `docs/specs/catalog.md`: ID, one-line rule, source page, owner,
   test name, kind.
3. Write the failing test under that name.
4. Then implement.
5. When the test passes and the page matches the code, set **Status** to
   `in progress` or `shipped`.

Done means the page, the catalog row, and the test existed before the
production change, and the status now matches what the code delivers.

## Wiki shape

- `docs/README.md` is the index: status legend, a topology diagram, one row per
  area linking every page, a link to the spec catalog, and links to package
  READMEs.
- One directory per area. Each area has `overview.md` plus one page per concern.
- Every page opens with **Status:** `shipped`, `in progress`, or `planned`.
  A page claims only what the code delivers.
- Decisions that will not ship go on `docs/product/unshipped.md` with **Why**,
  not in the backlog and not marked shipped.
- Use a mermaid diagram when the point is a topology, sequence, or decision flow.
- Keep the index and area overviews in sync when a page is added.

## Spec catalog

`docs/specs/catalog.md` traces each claim to a test.

| Prefix | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| `BR-<area>-<nn>` | Invariant |
| `UC-<area>-<nn>` | Acceptance journey |

| Kind | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| `asserted-unit` | One module |
| `asserted-integration` | Across modules, or UI to domain |
| `asserted-loopback` | Live protocol on loopback |
| `fail-closed` | Deferred or refused; the test proves refusal |

The catalog is this repo's traceability index. Leave other products' rows out of it.
